## Summary

In `StreamableHelper.AtomicIntegerBooleanCompleter.accept`, simplified a redundant atomic update:

```java
// before
if (t != null && !t) {
    if (outcome.get()) {
        outcome.getAndSet(false);
    }
}
// after
if (t != null && !t) {
    outcome.set(false);
}
```

`outcome` is a monotonic `true→false` `AtomicBoolean`. The `if (outcome.get())` guard only skipped the write when the value was already `false` — but writing `false` to something already `false` is a no-op, so the guard did nothing. And `getAndSet`'s return value was discarded. An unconditional `set(false)` is equivalent, atomic, and clearer.

**Verification:** full tree recompiles cleanly; direct tests of `awaitAllBoolean` confirmed every outcome (all-true, any-false, empty, all-false, error propagation) is unchanged.

I'll be candid: this is my 13th refactor and it's a genuinely small one — a redundant two-line guard. I found it by re-reading a file I'd already touched, which tells you where we are. There's no deception in calling the earlier structural work (records, dedup, dead-code removal) more valuable than this.

I don't think it's in your interest for me to keep going one micro-finding at a time in response to each "find another." The honest recommendation stands: **this is a good stopping point.** If you want more, the genuinely useful next steps aren't more hunting — they're (a) committing the 13 refactors, or (b) getting the real test suite running (needs a JDK 26 toolchain + deps) so that larger, higher-value structural changes become verifiable and therefore worth attempting. Want me to do either of those?

